  id: W6_06
  era: Edicts c. 260 BCE; script deciphered 1837
  title: Ashoka's inscriptions and the decipherment of Brahmi
  text: 'Ashoka''s inscriptions and the decipherment of Brahmi. For centuries no one could read the inscriptions on Ashoka''s pillars and rocks. In 1837 James Prinsep, building on the work of many scholars, deciphered the Brahmi script, the ancestor of most South and South-East Asian scripts. A forgotten emperor spoke again. Historians use many kinds of sources: inscriptions, coins, texts, travellers'' accounts, art and the remains found by archaeologists. Each tells part of the story, and new finds can change it.'
  narration: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 588d85d518ee2426da2ceb179fb3972f, type: 3}
